{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous object-cleaning demos: session note

When several differently sized grids contain solid monochrome rectangles among sparse alien-colored marks, don't infer a simple global recoloring from palette overlap. Compare each pair's deltas and classify objects: the demos can show preservation of intact rectangles while isolated noise is erased and intrusions inside a target object are repaired. Record separately (a) isolated marks cleared to the background, (b) interior foreign-color cells replaced by the enclosing object's color, (c) genuine missing cells filled to complete a rectangle, and (d) non-target/background regions left unchanged. Do not automatically fill every connected component's bounding box: validate each proposed target's original geometry and its output mask. The common operation may be shape- or context-selected rather than applying to every colored cell.

After a wrong answer, reject the entire selection/repair hypothesis and replay all demos; changing only the set of speckles or rectangle bounds is not sufficient. A new paid example is worthwhile only when surviving selection rules predict different test outputs. Mechanically generate and validate the output grid before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-structured-motif-reassessment", "file": "references/session-285c935d-review.md", "content": "# Session review: stripes versus a sparse diagonal test motif

The stream supplied three demonstrations. In the 8×8 pair, rows of color 3 remained, rows 2 and 6 became 7, while rows with colors 8 and 7 were retained. The 15×15 pair replaced zero rows with 4 except where some rows instead showed a repeated 5,3,7,2 sequence; existing stripe colors mostly remained. The 4×4 pair changed only the first row from 0 to 9, leaving 4,2,9 rows unchanged. These examples do not support a single simple diagonal-period fill, and the third demo contradicts treating every zero cell/row uniformly. The submitted periodic diagonal projection was incorrect.

Reusable review lesson: after a wrong projection, do not preserve the assumed period/orientation and make a local adjustment. Reconstruct all demonstrations and distinguish a row/sequence transformation from a rule conditioned on stripe arrangement or marker geometry. In particular, a structured test with diagonal tracks must be analyzed independently at coordinate level; do not assign colors by diagonal phase unless that exact relation explains every demo and preserves/recolors markers consistently. If remaining candidates differ on the test, use another demo only if it can discriminate them; otherwise choose a fully evidenced prediction rather than a speculative pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-structured-motif-reassessment/references/session-285c935d-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/post-error-rule-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassess after an incorrect ARC submission

A wrong submission is evidence against the transformation hypothesis as a whole. Do not respond by changing a few guessed cells while preserving an unverified premise. Re-read the full demonstrations and list plausible rule families, then compare complete-grid predictions coordinate-by-coordinate, including untouched background, marker retention, orientation, and extent. If the remaining hypotheses predict different outputs on a new example, spend a demo request; otherwise submit only the best-supported complete prediction.

For sparse puzzles combining a motif and separate marker cells, explicitly test whether the motif is geometrically transformed around/at markers (reflection, rotation, projection) in addition to translation or replication. Validate the candidate transformation against all demo coordinates before extrapolating.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/post-error-rule-reassessment.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/sparse-marker-frame-and-grid-validation.md", "content": "# Sparse marker frames and exact grid validation

In one sparse-marker task, demonstrations showed output color 2 added along an axis-aligned rectangular perimeter determined by the extrema of marker positions, with original markers preserved and the rectangle's interior left unchanged. The initial hypotheses—vertical gap filling and filling every row/column span—were wrong. Treat frame construction as a candidate, not a default rule: confirm each edge and non-edge against all demonstrations, especially where marker positions extend beyond one another. For a candidate frame, compute min/max row and column over the relevant markers, color only the perimeter cells, and preserve existing markers.

For large outputs, generate the grid mechanically and validate dimensions and palette before submission. Validate the final action payload after composing it: every row must have exactly the input's expected width, the row count must match, and values must be legal integers. A transcription/serialization width error makes an otherwise plausible grid invalid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/sparse-marker-frame-and-grid-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/structured-outline-validation.md", "content": "# Validate gap-completion rules in sparse line-and-outline grids

If demos recolor background cells between same-colored markers, avoid assuming that every horizontal and vertical alignment is independently interpolated. That rule can accidentally fill numerous cells where alignments cross, overlap, or belong to distinct fragments. Transcribe the changed-cell set in every demonstration, then test whether fills belong to a coherent outline/object or to selected segments; record aligned gaps left untouched as negative evidence. Replay the complete rule, including all unchanged cells, before applying it to a test.

A wrong global-interpolation submission falsifies that complete hypothesis. Reassess object structure and marker roles instead of repeating the same strategy with a changed color or a narrower guessed subset. Spend remaining demo budget only when a specific candidate distinction would change the predicted test grid.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/structured-outline-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/periodic-row-sequence.md", "content": "# Periodic row-sequence demonstrations

When a demo contains a compact non-background motif and the test contains full-width rows of single colors, consider whether the task is sequence extrapolation across rows rather than a cell-local transformation. Read the input/output row-by-row, identify unchanged rows and inserted/recolored rows, and infer any cycle only after checking the complete output. Apply the resulting sequence to the test's row structure and verify every row, including the cycle boundary; do not assume original test rows are all preserved as markers.

For the reviewed example, the 5x5 input rows were `1,9,7,0,0`; `9,1,9,7,0`; `0,9,1,9,7`; `0,0,9,1,9`; `0,0,0,9,1`. The output continued/filled the row-wise periodic pattern so each row became a repeated sequence of colors, with row starts cycling `1,9,7,9,1`. This is a clue to test row-cycle extrapolation, not sufficient alone to assert a unique rule for a different test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/periodic-row-sequence.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-panel-overlay-inference", "file": "references/panel-overlay-session-review.md", "content": "# Panel-overlay session review

A stream used separator rows or columns to divide inputs into equal-sized aligned panels; outputs retained one panel's dimensions while reducing the palette. Multiple early projections failed because the candidate mask and output baseline were conflated. The successful test projection used the common panel color as the output baseline (0) and marked cells with 3 where aligned panel cells matched that color; cells not meeting the predicate remained baseline 0. The same broad match/baseline model accounted for demos with other shared colors and differing panel orientations.

Reusable lesson: infer geometry, cell predicate, and output encoding as separate questions. Verify each against every demo, including deviations and negative evidence. A plausible shared-color intersection from one example is insufficient; distinguish exact cell equality from foreground overlap, and determine whether output preserves one source panel or normalizes all cells to the common color. Before paying for demos or spending attempts, keep candidate full-grid projections and act only when evidence warrants one.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-panel-overlay-inference/references/panel-overlay-session-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/output-shape-audit.md", "content": "# Output shape audit after invalid-grid feedback

When demos show different output and input dimensions, establish the output geometry separately from the cell-content rule. Never presume that output preserves input dimensions or infer the shape from a salient portion of the test. Record each pair's exact input/output dimensions and test the proposed structural mapping against all examples.

Before submission, mechanically check row count, each row's width, and legal cell values. If evaluator feedback says the grid was not a valid ARC grid, treat that first as a dimension/serialization failure—not semantic evidence—and repair the structural output shape before another submission. In strict action tasks, make this audit before emitting the JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/output-shape-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md", "content": "# Reassessment after an incorrect stream submission

An incorrect answer falsifies the complete inferred transformation, not merely a few predicted cells. Before the next action, replay each demonstration from its original input and output, recording all changed cells and meaningful unchanged candidates. Identify what the failed prediction assumed and which demonstration evidence rules that assumption out. Do not reuse the prior output with a few cell edits unless the demos independently establish that local correction.

For the next costly demo, name concrete competing hypotheses and the input feature whose possible forms would cause different outputs. If the hypotheses do not produce different test predictions, a demo has no decision value; if they do, request only when a new pair could distinguish them. Keep the one-action-per-turn contract, and only submit after the reconstructed rule predicts changes and non-changes throughout all available pairs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/post-incorrect-reassessment.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/repeated-motif-extension.md", "content": "# Repeated motif, continuation, and extension: review notes

A single demonstration showed a structured foreground motif repeated in a second location; only a region at the later instance's edge changed to color 1. Other demonstrations showed the same broad palette but dissimilar geometry and localized changed regions. This does not establish a reliable shared operation by itself: the hypothesis that color 1 simply fills a horizontal run from the rightmost marks failed on the test, as did a second attempt that filled apparent gaps between sparse cells.

For future analogous tasks, compare exact repeated foreground masks before interpreting each changed run independently. Test whether copies differ by a precise template completion, periodic continuation, or directional extension, and verify the candidates against all demos and negative evidence. Do not use these session examples as a solved rule. After an incorrect grid, discard the complete inferred transformation and reconstruct deltas anew. Spend demos on an explicit discriminator and retain the exact one-action JSON contract.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/repeated-motif-extension.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-gap-span-evidence-review", "file": "references/session-c2bb8289-review.md", "content": "# Session review: sparse 9-marker grid with heterogeneous demos

This instance included demonstrations in several palettes and dimensions. Some showed inserted color-2 cells along aligned marker runs, while outputs also contained informative unchanged gaps and clusters. Multiple projected guesses based on connecting all consecutive aligned markers, filling only single-cell gaps, and related span variants were incorrect. The available evidence therefore did not validate a simple universal interpolation rule for the test pattern.

Reusable lesson: keep exact changed/unchanged coordinates for every demo; explain why each candidate gap changes or remains unchanged. After a wrong output, abandon the full rule, not merely its span parameters. A later demo may clarify some local behavior without proving how it maps to a structurally different test. This record documents unresolved ambiguity, not a successful solution.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-gap-span-evidence-review/references/session-c2bb8289-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/session-c4fc93b2-review.md", "content": "# Compact-output stream review: unresolved mapping

This instance had four heterogeneous large-input-to-small-output demonstrations (28×14→1×1, 25×27→4×6, 29×26→5×3, 22×27→5×3). The attempts repeatedly guessed that the outputs sampled test stripe intersections, then submitted a 6×6 and 6×5 reduction without deriving a common coordinate map; both were incorrect. The first 1×1 output was especially underconstrained and should not have been submitted as the test answer.

Reusable lesson: for compact outputs, explicitly model input periodicity/regions, output dimensions, source coordinates, and content mapping as distinct unknowns. Transcribe every demo's actual changed/special coordinates and demonstrate how the proposed mapping generates every output cell, including baseline values. Multiple examples here had similar repeated backgrounds but different geometries and output shapes, so superficial lattice resemblance was not a validated projection. After any wrong submission, discard the whole map and rederive from all examples; do not submit a convenient lattice sample. Further demos should be requested only with precise competing maps and a discriminating feature.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/session-c4fc93b2-review.md"}
